---
/* How red gates arrive — for "Never believe the agent".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The article's second section is a distribution, so the drawing is one: runs
   as rows, gate results as cells in time order. Most rows never see a red at
   all; the row with something structural wrong sees several, together; and
   one row is red every time it ran — the false start, honestly labelled,
   which without gates would be a portfolio piece with an asterisk.

   The rows are schematic, not the corpus's: which runs, and how many, is in
   the strip where it is recomputed. Shape and clustering are the argument.
   Cells are colour as status — the one job semantic colour is allowed. */

type Cell = "g" | "r";
const ROWS: { label: string; note: string; cells: Cell[] }[] = [
  {
    label: "most runs",
    note: "never a red at all — which proves nothing on its own",
    cells: ["g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g"]
  },
  {
    label: "another",
    note: "the same, session after session",
    cells: ["g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g", "g"]
  },
  {
    label: "something structural wrong",
    note: "the reds arrive together — an environment that will not build, tests broken before any agent arrived",
    cells: ["g", "g", "r", "r", "r", "r", "g", "r", "r", "g", "g", "g"]
  },
  {
    label: "the false start",
    note: "red every time it ran, and it closed nothing — the gates are why it is labelled honestly",
    cells: ["r", "r", "r", "r", "r"]
  }
];
---

<figure class="fig fig-cluster">
  <div
    class="pane"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Four runs drawn as rows of gate results in time order. Two rows are green from end to end. One row holds a cluster of red results in the middle — a run with something structural wrong, like an environment that will not build. The last row is short and red in every cell: the false start, which closed nothing and is honestly labelled a failure only because the gates kept saying so."
  >
    <p class="fig-label">a corpus of gate results, run by run</p>
    <ul class="runs" aria-hidden="true">
      {
        ROWS.map((row) => (
          <li class="run">
            <span class="r-label">{row.label}</span>
            <span class="cells">
              {row.cells.map((cell) => (
                <span class={`cell ${cell === "r" ? "is-red" : "is-green"}`} />
              ))}
            </span>
            <span class="r-note">{row.note}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    Agents do not fail continuously and they do not fail never — the reds
    cluster in the runs where something structural is wrong. A checking system
    earns its keep on the bottom two rows, and looks like overhead on the top
    two.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .runs {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-s);
  }

  .run {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  .r-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .cells {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  /* Small enough to read as a distribution, big enough to count. Bordered in
     the hairline so a green cell and the mantle behind it stay separable in
     both schemes. */
  .cell {
    inline-size: 1rem;
    block-size: 1rem;
    border: var(--rule);
  }

  .cell.is-green {
    background: var(--green);
  }

  .cell.is-red {
    background: var(--red);
  }

  .r-note {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
